	"about": "How Chinese verb forms are made and turned into lessons. Written by Claude (Opus) with Ben on 16 November 2026, the first language whose verbs never change. There is no table in the dictionary to read, because there is nothing to conjugate: who is doing it is the word in front (我, 你, 她), and when, still going and telling are little words around the verb (了 after it for done, 在 in front for going on, 会 for later, 别 for don't, 吧 for let's). So `built` makes each form from a pattern, and the dump is asked only whether the word is a verb and for its pinyin, which a table shows in brackets beside each form (吃了 (chī le)) and the Play button leaves out. A simplified word that the dump only redirects (睡觉 to 睡覺) is followed to the entry that has the sound. 不 is read bú before a fourth tone. Verbs made of a verb and its object (唱歌, sing a song) take 过 inside them (唱过歌), and the pinyin cannot be split there by rule, so they have no 'ever' form yet. The lessons have no pinyin: the board has none for any Chinese lesson until the sounds work is done.",
